# worldstream: the noodle shop

A pixel-art noodle shop, open late, streamed on Kick through an OBS browser
source. One building, one keeper. He cooks, serves, checks the mail, sweeps the
step, straightens the board when it leans, kicks the vending machine when it
flickers, and goes up to the balcony when the top lantern goes out. Chat comes
in, gets a bowl, hangs about. Visiting the stream is visiting the shop.

There is no storyline, no travelling, no events. Small things happen and he
deals with them.

## Run

```
npm install
npm run dev          # packs the art, rebuilds the renderer on change, runs the server on :4400
npm run chat         # mock chat REPL: "mika: !order miso" speaks as mika; /mod toggles the moderator badge
```

- OBS: add a **Browser** source, URL `http://localhost:4400/`, 1920x1080, 60 fps. `?hud=1` adds a debug line.
- Sound: the page plays a quiet lo-fi loop with Web Audio (no files). Tick **Control audio via OBS** in the source properties. `?nomusic` disables it; the admin page has on/off and volume, saved in the state.
- Operator page: `http://localhost:4400/admin` (localhost only): live state, recent chat with what happened to each line, weather, knock things over, spawn a passer-by or the cat, drop his current task, put words in his mouth.
- Fixed scene without chat, for art checks: `http://localhost:4400/?demo=1&weather=rain&spot=door&mode=sweep&bin=fallen&bubble=hello`. Spots and modes are the ids in `src/shared/scene.ts` and `src/shared/state.ts`.

## Chat

Commands work at once. Everything else may go to the brain (below).

| Command | What happens |
|---|---|
| `!order [dish]`, `!ramen`, `!udon` | a customer with your name walks in and takes a stool; he cooks, serves, the bowl steams, they leave when it is empty |
| `!menu` | what is on tonight |
| `!hi` | he waves and says hello back |
| `!fix bin` (or `board`, `vending`, `mail`, `plants`, `lantern`, `sign`), `!bin` | you point at something that is off; he goes and sorts it, and thanks you |
| `!flag [red\|white\|blue\|yellow] <words>` | regulars only: your banner stands by the kerb, up to 10 letters, for a few hours. `!flag remove` takes it down |
| `!cat` | friends only: a cat comes to live at the shop for a while |
| `!tab` | your standing: bowls, nights, points |
| `!weather rain`, `!say ...`, `!knock bin` | moderators |

**He asks back.** A few seconds after an order lands, a hello, a useful
alert, a flag or the cat, he follows up with one small question for that
viewer ("long day, mika?", "where are you tuning in from?", "does it have a
name?"), remembers he asked, and treats their next line as the answer: the
brain replies to what they said, or without the brain he acknowledges from a
bank. Answering earns a point. One question per person every eight minutes,
never two to chat within 45 seconds (`questions` in the config).

**He asks the street.** When nobody has said anything for five minutes he asks
the whole room something anyone can answer ("what's everyone eating tonight?",
"rain here. what's it like where you are?"). The first answer closes it and the
brain is told who answered so it can reply to them and pull others in. At most
one every eight minutes, none while he naps or when Kick says nobody is
watching. If he has dozed off on the stool, the first person to speak wakes him.

**Rapport.** Every conversation earns a point (at most one every 30 seconds),
every bowl five, every greeting two, every useful alert three, every new night
two. At 15 you are a regular and can hang a flag; at 40 you are a friend and
can bring the cat (`rapport` in the config). Moderators skip the gates.

**The brain.** Plain chat is batched and read by Claude (`nl.mode` in the
config: `all`, `mention` or `off`; needs `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in `.env`). It
speaks as the keeper and acts only through tools that turn into the same
commands as above, so the cooldowns, the menu and the rapport gates apply the
same way. It sees the scene (weather, what he is doing, who is at the counter,
what is broken) and short true notes about the people talking.

## What he does on his own

Idle jobs are picked by weight and cooldown (`src/server/jobs.ts`): tending the
pot (most of the time), wiping the counter, sweeping the step (more when it
snows), looking down the street, a sit on the end stool with tea (a nap when
chat has been quiet), a look from the balcony, watering the plants, feeding the
cat, bringing a delivery in, getting a can from the machine. He waves at
passers-by when he is free.

Mishaps arrive every few minutes (`street.mishapEveryMs`): the bin goes over,
the board leans, the vending machine flickers, the plants wilt, the top lantern
goes out, the neon glitches; mail comes now and then. He notices on his own
after a while (`keeper.noticeMs`), or straight away when chat points. The cat,
when there is one, wanders between its spots and now and then knocks the bin
over itself.

Weather drifts between clear, rain, snow and fog on the configured odds. It is
always night.

## Kick

1. Create an app at kick.com/settings/developer (2FA required). Redirect URL:
   `http://localhost:4400/kick/callback`. Enable webhooks and set the webhook
   URL to your public tunnel plus `/kick/webhook`.
2. Put `KICK_CLIENT_ID`, `KICK_CLIENT_SECRET` and `KICK_WEBHOOK_PUBLIC_URL` in `.env`.
3. Expose the server: `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:4400` (or any HTTPS tunnel).
4. Start the server and open `http://localhost:4400/kick/login` while logged into Kick.
   Tokens land in `state/kick-tokens.json`; subscriptions are created and re-checked on
   every boot. `/kick/status` shows the connection.

Webhooks are verified against Kick's published RSA key and de-duplicated. Set
`kick.botRepliesEnabled` (or flip it on the admin page) to echo his lines into
chat as the bot.

## Layout

- `src/shared/` the scene (every coordinate: spots, fixtures, stools, flag slots), state types and the pure reducer, the command parser, the catalogue types, config schema, rapport maths
- `src/server/` HTTP + WebSocket server, store, the keeper engine and his jobs, the order flow, the street (weather, passers-by, mishaps, the cat), the interpreter, the regulars ledger, the brain, Kick, admin
- `src/web/` Canvas 2D renderer: 480x270 internal, scaled x4; sky and mountains, the building sprites, the keeper, lights, weather, bubbles and flags, music
- `assets/shop/` `palette.txt`, `catalogue.json` (sprites, menu, line banks, weather odds), `sprites/*.sprite`, `keeper/*.sprite`
- `assets/font.txt` the 5x7 bitmap font
- `scripts/rig/` the art is generated: `shop.ts` paints the building, `people.ts` the keeper, passers-by and customers, `props.ts` the fixtures, bowl and cat; `preview.ts` composes `dist/shop/preview.png` without a browser
- `scripts/pack.ts` validates every pixel against the palette and writes `dist/shop/atlas.json` + `sheet.png`
- `config/world.config.json` the shop's name and persona, limits and cooldowns, rapport thresholds, the keeper's timings, the street's odds, the brain, Kick
- `docs/ART.md` the style guide
- `state/` `shop.json`, `regulars.json`, `kick-tokens.json` (gitignored)

## Sprites

A sprite is a text grid over single-character palette keys, one file per sprite:

```
name: bowl
anchor: bottom
fps: 3
---
......v...
....m..v..
..kkkkkk..
.mmmmmmmmz
--
(next frame)
```

`N* row` repeats a row, short rows are padded, `--` separates frames, and
`npm run assets` refuses any pixel that is not a palette key. Most of the art is
written by the rigs; edit the rig and rerun `npm run rig`, then look at
`dist/shop/preview.png`.
